% Origin: Kowalski, *Computational Logic and Human Thinking*,
%         Chapter 4 "Logic and Computation", section 4.1
%         ("The party-looping example").
% Last verification: no open issues (no errors/warnings; all tests pass).
%
% The book's rules form a deliberate loop: "mary will go to the party if john
% will go to the party" and "john will go to the party if mary will go to the
% party". Neither mary nor john has an independent reason to go, so the loop
% yields nothing; only bob, asserted as a fact, will go. LE tabling terminates
% on the mutual recursion and returns just bob.
%
% Reconstruction: to keep the rules generic (LE flags rules built from bare
% constants), the "goes if so-and-so goes" dependency is lifted into a generic
% rule over a "follows" relation, and the concrete mary<->john loop is the pair
% of scenario facts "mary follows john" / "john follows mary". This is logically
% identical to the book's two clauses, while bob's unconditional attendance is a
% genuine knowledge-base fact (a generic person fact has no free variable to
% lift, so it stays in the KB as the book has it).

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
*a person* will go to the party.
*a first person* follows *a second person*.

the knowledge base party includes:

% A person will go to the party if someone they follow will go.
a first person will go to the party
    if the first person follows a second person
    and the second person will go to the party.

bob will go to the party.

scenario empty is:
    mary follows john.
    john follows mary.
    attendance expects answers ["bob will go to the party"].

query attendance is:
    which person will go to the party.
